NNAAMMEE
     aattrruunn — run jobs queued for later execution

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     aattrruunn [--ll _l_o_a_d___a_v_g] [--dd]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     AAttrruunn runs jobs queued by at(1).

     The system crontab(5) file _/_e_t_c_/_c_r_o_n_t_a_b must contain the line

     */5     *       *       *       *       root    /usr/libexec/atrun

     so aattrruunn is invoked every five minutes.

     At every invocation, aattrruunn starts all the jobs in the lowercase queues
     whose start time has elapsed.  In addition, if the load average over the
     last minute was less than the specified limit, then a maximum of one
     batch job (denoted by the uppercase queues) is started.

     Before starting a job, aattrruunn checks the status of its owner's account
     with pam(3) and refuses to run the job if the account is unavailable,
     e.g., locked out or expired.

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
     --ll _l_o_a_d___a_v_g
             Specify a limiting load factor, over which batch jobs should not
             be run, instead of the default of 1.5 * number of active CPUs.

     --dd      Debug; print error messages to standard error instead of using
             syslog(3).

WWAARRNNIINNGGSS
     For aattrruunn to work, a cron(8) daemon must be running aattrruunn periodically.

FFIILLEESS
     /etc/pam.d/atrun  pam.conf(5) configuration file for aattrruunn
     /var/at/jobs      Directory containing job files
     /var/at/spool     Directory containing output spool files

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     at(1), crontab(1), pam(3), syslog(3), crontab(5), pam.conf(5), cron(8)

BBUUGGSS
     The functionality of aattrruunn should be merged into cron(8).

